#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203744 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf03377-f06d-5831-80b8-dcbf4978a8db" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Positive Coefficients (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/id #uuid "f57cd9ee-7613-5083-948a-2434078d6796" :question/math-academy-id "e-15071" :question/problem "Factor the trinomial $-4w^2-30w-50.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-4w^2-30w-50\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms in the trinomial have a common factor of $-2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-2\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n-2(2w^2+15w+25)\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to factor the trinomial $2w^2+15w+25$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times 25 = 50$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($15$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $50.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $50$ are\n\n$$\n(1,50),\\quad (2,25),\\quad (5,10).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(5,10)$ add to $15{:}$ \n\n$$\n5+10 =15  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $5w+10w,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{w}^{2}+15w+25 & = \\\\ 2{w}^{2}+5w+10w+25 & = \\\\ 2{w}^{2}+10w+5w+25 & = \\\\ (2{w}^{2}+10w)+(5w+25) & = \\\\ 2w(w+5)+5(w+5) & = \\\\ (2w+5)(w+5) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(2w+5)(w+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203745 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b49cf980-1078-593b-bf56-c9a132c41273" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Negative Constants (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/id #uuid "c6a7913e-d53e-5885-b30c-87148e86032d" :question/math-academy-id "e-15088" :question/problem "Factor the trinomial $-10t^2 - 35t + 20.$" :question/worked-solution "We must factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-10t^2 - 35t +20\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $-5.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-5\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(2{t}^{2}+7t-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2t^2 + 7t - 4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2 \\times (-4) = -8$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($7$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-8$ are\n\n$$\n(1,-8),\\quad (-1,8),\\quad (2,-4),\\quad (-2,4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,8)$ add to $7{:}$ \n\n$$\n-1+8 = 8-1 = 7  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-t+8t,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}+7t-4 & = \\\\ 2{t}^{2}-t+8t-4 & = \\\\ (2{t}^{2}-t)+(8t-4) & = \\\\ t(2t-1)+4(2t-1) & = \\\\ (2t-1)(t+4) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(2t-1)(t+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203746 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e1a8957-9964-5eba-b5f4-b0e1dd525838" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/id #uuid "81afbe63-1358-5d90-999d-4797f5f44f78" :question/math-academy-id "e-15089" :question/problem "Factor the trinomial $15x^2-40x+20.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n15x^2-40x+20\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $5.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $5{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3{x}^{2}-8x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $3x^2 - 8x + 4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $3 \\times 4 = 12$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $-8$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $12.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-12),\\quad (-2,-6),\\quad (-3,-4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-2,-6)$ add to $-8{:}$ \n\n$$\n-2-6= -8  \\quad{\\color{green}\\checkmark}\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-2x-6x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-8x+4 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-2x-6x+4 & = \\\\ 3{x}^{2}-6x-2x+4 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}-6x)-(2x-4) & = \\\\ 3x(x-2)-2(x-2) & = \\\\ (3x-2)(x-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5(3x-2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204336 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf03377-f06d-5831-80b8-dcbf4978a8db" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Positive Coefficients (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "330ab0dd-02fd-56db-b804-f8f33e042f45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3(x+4)(2x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507669} {:answer/id #uuid "49cdacc0-415a-5700-bc99-a9255a76dac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+1)(4x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507710} {:answer/id #uuid "8072f33f-7cd1-5feb-ae96-8d19e31a9b4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+1)(2x+4)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507791} {:answer/id #uuid "81aa656d-a1b1-57e7-9c2a-b9c71123e89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+4)(3x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507792} {:answer/id #uuid "fa6ab955-2370-54a3-8338-4e9edcb85b58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+1)(x+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507938}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81aa656d-a1b1-57e7-9c2a-b9c71123e89c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x+4)(3x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507792} :answer-field/id #uuid "e52abdf3-c0dc-5ef1-894f-dee194666a8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200933}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6a1a0c7d-b300-5f97-894e-7b4ae6893dfe" :question/math-academy-id "q-225002" :question/problem "Factor $6{x}^{2}+26x+8.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n6{x}^{2}+26x+8\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $2:$\n\n$$\n2(3{x}^{2}+13x+4)\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the trinomial $3{x}^{2}+13x+4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3\\times 4=12$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($13$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $12.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $12$ are\n\n$$\n(1,12),\\,(3,4),\\,(6,2).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,12)$ add to $13:$ \n\n$$\n1+12=13\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $x+12x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}+13x+4= \\\\ 3{x}^{2}+x+12x+4 & = \\\\ (3{x}^{2}+x)+(12x+4) & = \\\\ x(3x+1)+4(3x+1) & = \\\\ (x+4)(3x+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(x+4)(3x+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508114 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203942 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "caf03377-f06d-5831-80b8-dcbf4978a8db" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Positive Coefficients (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d54aa99-4cfb-5814-bc10-9c3e358bb939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507655}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d54aa99-4cfb-5814-bc10-9c3e358bb939" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507655} :answer-field/id #uuid "61098ed3-6c01-5235-af85-f4569c7bd995" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507986} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6714948d-f4bf-512c-9062-e9a5e8a72d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6714948d-f4bf-512c-9062-e9a5e8a72d89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507750} :answer-field/id #uuid "71adbbc8-62c5-5837-96d6-ec82bf8d4c9d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507991} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "344a38fb-29cc-5038-93a9-d3e715d045f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "344a38fb-29cc-5038-93a9-d3e715d045f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507672} :answer-field/id #uuid "b52fda95-06a0-5c0b-8089-003053de76c4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508013}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f09d890b-8768-5b87-b720-b755f6cef560" :question/math-academy-id "q-225007" :question/problem "**Fully** factor the following trinomial.\n\n$6{u}^{2}+34u+20=$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$u\\,+2)(u\\,+$\n{{field-3}}$)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n6{u}^{2}+34u+20\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms in the trinomial have a common factor of $2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $2:$\n\n$$\n2(3{u}^{2}+17u+10)\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we need to factor the trinomial $3{u}^{2}+17u+10$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that\n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($3\\times 10=30$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($17$).\n\nSince all coefficients are positive, we only need to consider the positive factor pairs of $30.$\n\nThe positive factor pairs of $30$ are\n\n$$\n(1,30),\\,(2,15),\\,(3,10),\\,(6,5).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(2,15)$ add to $17:$ \n\n$$\n2+15=17\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $2u+15u,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{u}^{2}+17u+10 & = \\\\ 3{u}^{2}+2u+15u+10 & = \\\\ 3{u}^{2}+15u+2u+10 & = \\\\ (3{u}^{2}+15u)+(2u+10) & = \\\\ 3u(u+5)+2(u+5) & = \\\\ (3u+2)(u+5) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{u}^{2}+34u+20= \\\\ 2(3{u}^{2}+17u+10)= \\\\ 2(3u+2)(u+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e1a8957-9964-5eba-b5f4-b0e1dd525838" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13b8e1a5-5367-508e-ac8f-3841576356f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507621}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "13b8e1a5-5367-508e-ac8f-3841576356f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507621} :answer-field/id #uuid "90c7b183-e533-502b-8176-8388d1565702" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200939} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a20d43fc-e518-5c12-b7c1-9f2ff9d029af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a20d43fc-e518-5c12-b7c1-9f2ff9d029af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507828} :answer-field/id #uuid "17c3c1df-a7e2-5642-ad06-78f1c0578359" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200940} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "95cbedfb-d850-5f9f-8cf1-3a3819b2c147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507809}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "95cbedfb-d850-5f9f-8cf1-3a3819b2c147" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507809} :answer-field/id #uuid "7af37f00-7de9-5a1f-b9ee-080fd67e4675" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186200941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "98b7e6f2-e40d-535e-a12b-3e5bd59b4957" :question/math-academy-id "q-225073" :question/problem "**Fully** factor the following trinomial.\n\n$8{y}^{2}-36y+40=$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$y-5)(y\\,-$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n8{y}^{2}-36y+40\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $4.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2{y}^{2}-9y+10)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2{y}^{2}-9y+10$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $2\\times 10=20$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $-9$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the constant is positive, we need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $20.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $20$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-20),\\,(-2,-10),\\,(-4,-5).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-4,-5)$ add to $-9:$ \n\n$$\n-4-5=-9\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-4y-5y,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-9y+10 & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}-4y-5y+10 & = \\\\ (2{y}^{2}-4y)-(5y-10) & = \\\\ 2y(y-2)-5(y-2) & = \\\\ (2y-5)(y-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8{y}^{2}-36y+40 & = \\\\ 4(2{y}^{2}-9y+10) & = \\\\ 4(2y-5)(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203944 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e1a8957-9964-5eba-b5f4-b0e1dd525838" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Negative Linear Coefficients and Positive Constants (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30008591-dd3e-5a3b-b9ab-5089264ea73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(4x-2)(5x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507662} {:answer/id #uuid "30c2c881-2b11-5d0f-9a61-3ce3b4c82e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(5x-4)(5x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507666} {:answer/id #uuid "52487b66-b0a5-5f6d-b82f-bf4c0c501405" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4(5x-2)(5x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507721} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9fec6c-3b2d-5102-bdb2-f4ff9578dd4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(5x-4)(5x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507863} {:answer/id #uuid "ca4dc6c1-8e6a-5b06-aa3e-b775836dc199" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5(5x-4)(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30c2c881-2b11-5d0f-9a61-3ce3b4c82e9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(5x-4)(5x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507666} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b985e5a-2dcc-5af7-a7eb-c2aa1abf5738" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507948}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ded8bac7-351c-5c3f-9827-3494bc9b4c87" :question/math-academy-id "q-225155" :question/problem "$-50{x}^{2}+50x-8=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-50{x}^{2}+50x-8\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $-2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(25{x}^{2}-25x+4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $25{x}^{2}-25x+4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $25\\times 4=100$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $-25$).\n\nSince the linear coefficient is negative and the constant is positive, we only need to consider the *negative* factor pairs of $100.$\n\nThe negative factor pairs of $100$ are\n\n$$\n(-1,-100),\\,(-2,-50),\\,(-4,-25),\\,(-5,-20),\\,(-10,-10).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-5,-20)$ add to $-25:$ \n\n$$\n-5-20=-25\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-5x-20x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25{x}^{2}-25x+4 & = \\\\ 25{x}^{2}-5x-20x+4 & = \\\\ (25{x}^{2}-5x)-(20x-4) & = \\\\ 5x(5x-1)-4(5x-1) & = \\\\ (5x-4)(5x-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(5x-4)(5x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508073 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b49cf980-1078-593b-bf56-c9a132c41273" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Negative Constants (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35a5f395-d8a6-58f5-9342-209d117eb19f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4(x+4)(2x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507676} {:answer/id #uuid "3918933e-05fa-5e6b-bc82-99cc88135328" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(x-4)(4x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507679} {:answer/id #uuid "7959d810-30e7-5428-b189-92de0ce52f62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4(x+4)(x-5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507774} {:answer/id #uuid "9398dd9d-12b5-526b-ad13-80a36d1000dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(x+4)(4x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507806} {:answer/id #uuid "af84559d-6b58-577f-a57d-dfd85e961ec8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(x+4)(4x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507841}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9398dd9d-12b5-526b-ad13-80a36d1000dc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2(x+4)(4x-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507806} :answer-field/id #uuid "f08665b6-f5cb-5dcf-be72-bcce09476082" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508028}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78ded0e2-d300-5bb0-ad2a-e4c3015aff51" :question/math-academy-id "q-225265" :question/problem "$-8{x}^{2}-30x+8=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n-8{x}^{2}-30x+8\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $-2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(4{x}^{2}+15x-4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $4{x}^{2}+15x-4$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant (which is $4\\times (-4)=-16$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient (which is $15$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-16$  are\n\n$$\n(1,-16),\\,(-1,16),\\,(2,-8),\\,(-2,8),\\,(4,-4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(-1,16)$ add to $15:$ \n\n$$\n-1+16=15\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $-x+16x,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}+15x-4 & = \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-x+16x-4 & = \\\\ (4{x}^{2}-x)+(16x-4) & = \\\\ x(4x-1)+4(4x-1) & = \\\\ (x+4)(4x-1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2(x+4)(4x-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508054 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203943 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b49cf980-1078-593b-bf56-c9a132c41273" :knowledge-point/title "Quadratics With Negative Constants (After Initial Factoring)"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0affc26b-60f0-51d5-b33d-6f529043658e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(w+4)(2w+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507613} {:answer/id #uuid "3b0df99f-a245-56e9-b46b-60f41afcd57d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(w-4)(2w+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507683} {:answer/id #uuid "428ea5d8-7358-534a-91fe-ee51f15e5d68" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(w+8)(2w-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507700} {:answer/id #uuid "708da6c0-b070-54e6-9d7e-94f592d3e0d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(w+4)(4w+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507760} {:answer/id #uuid "c16347d3-02fb-5ca1-b434-258c9314d35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(w-8)(2w+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507867}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c16347d3-02fb-5ca1-b434-258c9314d35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(w-8)(2w+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186507867} :answer-field/id #uuid "8e07d7d3-1dca-5770-910c-195812db5214" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186508003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "30760314-ac6d-51dc-a95d-a6b32fc33073" :question/math-academy-id "q-225283" :question/problem "$4{w}^{2}-30w-16=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to factor the following expression:\n\n$$\n4{w}^{2}-30w-16\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that all the terms have a common factor of $2.$ So, we can simplify the calculation by first factoring out $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(2{w}^{2}-15w-8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to factor the trinomial $2{w}^{2}-15w-8$ in parentheses. To do this, we find two numbers that \n\n- multiply to the product of the quadratic coefficient and the constant ($2\\times -8=-16$), and\n\n- add to the linear coefficient ($-15$).\n\nThe factor pairs of $-16$ are\n\n$$\n(1,-16),\\,(-1,16),\\,(2,-8),\\,(-2,8),\\,(4,-4).\n$$\n\nNotice the numbers in the factor pair $(1,-16)$ add to $-15:$ \n\n$$\n1-16=-15\\,✓\n$$\n\nThen, we write the linear term as $w-16w,$ and factor the result by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{w}^{2}-15w-8 & = \\\\ 2{w}^{2}+w-16w-8 & = \\\\ (2{w}^{2}+w)-(16w+8) & = \\\\ w(2w+1)-8(2w+1) & = \\\\ (w-8)(2w+1) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the original trinomial factors as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(w-8)(2w+1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
